.TH THERMO 2
.SH NAME
thermo: fromgamma, statept, isentropic, polytropic, carnot, otto, diesel, brayton, vanderwaals, lmtd, effectiveness \- gases, processes, cycles and heat exchange
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "thermo.m";
thermo := load Thermo Thermo->PATH;
Gas, State, Change, Cycle, VdW: import thermo;

thermo->init();
g := thermo->air();
(s, err) := thermo->statept(g, 100000.0, 300.0);
(c, err) := thermo->isentropic(g, s, 1000000.0);
(cy, err) := thermo->otto(g, 9.0, 300.0, 100000.0, 1e6);
(dt, err) := thermo->lmtd(Thermo->COUNTER, thi, tho, tci, tco);
(e, err) := thermo->effectiveness(Thermo->COUNTER, ntu, cr);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Thermo
is an ideal gas with constant specific heats, the processes it can
undergo, the power cycles built from those, a real gas, and the two
standard ways of sizing a heat exchanger.
.SS Two conventions to get straight first
Heat added
.B to
the system is positive and work done
.B by
the system is positive, so the first law reads d\fIu\fP =
.IR q " \- " w
and an engine that takes heat in and does work has both positive.
The other convention exists, mostly in chemistry, and every number here
would change sign under it.
.PP
Units are SI and
.BR absolute .
Temperature is in kelvin,
.B never
celsius \- a ratio of temperatures is meaningless otherwise, and every
efficiency here is a ratio of temperatures.
A negative absolute temperature is refused, which is what catches a
celsius value passed by mistake.
Pressure is in pascals; volume, energy and entropy are per kilogram.
.SS Gases
.B Fromgamma
and
.B fromcp
each build a gas from the molar mass and
.I one
other number, because
.IR cp " \- " cv " = " R
is a relation and not a definition: being allowed to state all three
would be being allowed to state an impossible gas.
.B Air
is the one everybody wants.
.SS States and processes
.BR Statepv ,
.B statept
and
.B statevt
fix a state from any two properties and construct the rest.
.PP
.BR Isothermal ,
.BR isobaric ,
.BR isochoric ,
.B isentropic
and the general
.B polytropic
each return the work done and the heat added, and each computes the two
.I independently
\- the work from the process and the heat from the process, neither from
the other.
That makes the first law something
.IR thermotest (1)
can check rather than something this code assumes.
.PP
.B Polytropic
takes the index directly:
.I n
= 1 is isothermal,
.I n
= gamma is isentropic,
.I n
= 0 is isobaric, and an index between 1 and gamma describes a
compression that both heats the gas and rejects heat, which is the case
that catches a sign error in the polytropic specific heat.
.SS Cycles
.BR Carnot ,
.BR otto ,
.B diesel
and
.B brayton
each return the efficiency and the work and heat around the loop, so
that a caller can check the first law rather than taking the efficiency
on trust.
.PP
Their efficiencies are 1 \-
.IR Tc / Th ,
1 \-
.IR r \u\s-2(1\-gamma)\s+2\d,
and 1 \-
.IR rp \u\s-2((1\-gamma)/gamma)\s+2\d.
The Otto and Brayton figures depend on the compression or pressure ratio
.I alone
\- adding four times the heat to an Otto cycle does not change its
efficiency at all, which is the surprising part of the result and is
checked rather than repeated.
.SS A real gas
.B Vanderwaals
builds
.IR p " = " RT /(\fIv\fP\-\fIb\fP) \- \fIa\fP/\fIv\fP\u\s-22\s+2\d
from the critical temperature and pressure, for the one thing an ideal
gas cannot do at all: have a critical point and condense.
.B VdW.critical
returns that point and
.B VdW.z
the compressibility factor, which is 1 for an ideal gas and exactly 3/8
at the critical point of
.I any
van der Waals gas.
.SS Heat transfer
.B Finefficiency
is tanh(\fImL\fP)/\fImL\fP, which goes to one for a short fat fin and to
zero for a long thin one.
.PP
.B Lmtd
is the log mean temperature difference, which a designer uses when both
outlet temperatures are known;
.B effectiveness
is the effectiveness-NTU relation, used when they are not.
They are different arithmetic for one duty and must agree.
At equal capacities the counterflow formula divides by zero and the
limit
.IR NTU /(1+\fINTU\fP)
is taken instead.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/thermo.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR flow (2),
.IR pde (2),
.IR thermotest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All of these return a diagnostic string, nil on success: a gamma outside
the range any gas has, a negative absolute temperature or pressure, a
cold reservoir hotter than the hot one, a compression ratio below one, a
cutoff ratio above the compression ratio, a turbine inlet cooler than
the compressor outlet, two streams that cross in an exchanger.
.SH BUGS
The gas is ideal and its specific heats are constant.
Real air's cp rises by about a fifth between 300 K and 1500 K, so every
cycle temperature computed here for an engine is out by tens of kelvin
and every efficiency is optimistic.
This is the model a first course works in and it should not be used to
size anything.
.PP
There is no working substance that changes phase, so no Rankine cycle,
no refrigeration cycle with a real refrigerant, and no steam anywhere.
That needs property tables, which are data rather than formulas and are
not here.
.PP
Nothing is irreversible.
Every compressor and turbine is perfect, and there is no isentropic
efficiency to spoil them with.
.PP
.B Vanderwaals
is used for the critical point and the compressibility factor only.
Below the critical temperature its isotherm has the unphysical loop that
a Maxwell construction exists to cut out, and nothing here cuts it out;
asking for the pressure in that region gives the loop.
